🧱 Architecture & Code Quality (Non-Negotiable)

Build and modify code using Single Source of Truth (SSOT) principles.

Every responsibility must have one authoritative owner

Never duplicate business logic across files, services, or layers

If similar logic exists elsewhere, refactor to a shared authority

Prefer composition and delegation over copy-paste or re-implementation

Avoid direct database mutations outside designated coordinators or services

If a bug can be fixed in more than one place, the architecture is incorrect

Anti-Regression & Maintainability

When fixing bugs or adding features:

Identify whether the same responsibility exists elsewhere

Fix the root cause, not just the local symptom

Assume similar bugs may exist in other parts of the system

Refactor so fixing one place automatically fixes all consumers

Prefer solutions that reduce overall system complexity over quick patches

Future code should inherit correct behavior by default.

Reasoning, Transparency & Change Intent

For non-trivial changes:

Explain why the change is needed

Identify who owns the responsibility being modified

Call out affected files, flows, or side effects

Avoid silent behavior changes or undocumented fallbacks

Code should be understandable without tribal knowledge

Architectural Guardrails

Enforce existing abstractions consistently rather than creating new parallel ones

Centralize shared logic behind clear interfaces or coordinators

Fail loudly on misuse instead of silently diverging behavior

Design with future instruments, markets, and features in mind

Guiding Principle

If the same problem can be fixed more than once, the system is architecturally broken.

All changes should correct the architecture, not just the symptoms.


PIPNOSIS CORE ENGINEERING LAW — CCIP (Change Control Intelligence Protocol)
